    "exampleHtml": "a <span class=\"vp-verb\">lovely</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">old wooden</span> table · a <span class=\"vp-prep\">big red</span> bus · a <span class=\"vp-verb\">nice</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">new</span> phone · a <span class=\"vp-prep\">small Italian</span> café",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Opinion first.</strong> Words like <em>lovely, nice, horrible, comfortable</em> say what you think. They come before the facts: <em>a lovely old house</em>, not <em>an old lovely house</em>.",
        "tag": "Opinion before fact"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Facts: size, age, shape, colour, origin, material.</strong> A small helper is the word <em>OSASCOMP</em> (Opinion, Size, Age, Shape, Colour, Origin, Material, Purpose). <em>a big old car, a small round table, a black leather jacket, a new French film</em>.",
        "tag": "The usual order"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Two or three adjectives</strong> are normal. Four or five sound heavy. Say <em>a lovely old wooden table</em> and stop there. Do not put a comma between adjectives of different types: <em>a big red bus</em>, not <em>a big, red bus</em>.",
        "tag": "Not too many"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>After <em>be</em></strong> the order is freer, and you join the last two with <em>and</em>: <em>The house is old and beautiful. The room was big, bright and warm.</em>",
        "tag": "After be"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>a wooden old table</em> → ✓ <em>an old wooden table</em> · ✗ <em>a red big car</em> → ✓ <em>a big red car</em> · ✗ <em>two blues bags</em> / <em>a car red</em> → ✓ <em>two blue bags</em> / <em>a red car</em> (adjectives come before the noun and never take <em>-s</em>)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Learners often put the material or colour too early. A good rule: the word that tells <em>what it is made of</em> or <em>what type it is</em> stays closest to the noun: <em>a leather bag</em>, <em>a wooden chair</em>. A purpose word is really a noun and stands right next to the noun: <em>a running shoe, a sleeping bag</em>. More adjectives: <a href=\"/ed-ing-adjectives\">-ed and -ing adjectives</a> (<em>a boring old film</em>) and <a href=\"/the-adjective-one\">the + adjective, one / ones</a>.",
